## `12` exits 1, and that is the point

It is the only attachment that does not exit 0. It runs round 3's payload `876f66f` with its own
unmodified validator in a worktree — which is what a clone contains — and it exits 1 with publication
hygiene CONTAMINATED at 0.6667. Round 3's committed `validation-03/02-npm-run-validate-e0.txt` records
the same command at the same commit exiting 0 with CLEAN at 0.4444.

Both are honest reports of their own machine. They disagree because round 3's corpus was every file on
disk under three roots, and the producing machine held two gitignored JSON build artifacts under
`runs/` whose vocabulary was keeping a generated `package.json`'s words below the distinctiveness
cutoff. Two files nobody committed were the difference between a pass and a fail. That is why round 4
defines the corpus over tracked files and gives it an identity.

Nothing in round 3's record is edited. It stays exactly as committed, describing exactly what it
described.

## Redaction in `01-npm-run-check.txt`

`npm run check` runs the local-p0x corpus validators, whose test names print private evaluation-case
identifiers. Those are sealed material, which the methodology's "separate public diagnostic fixtures
from sealed case material" keeps out of published records, so 21 case identifiers and 1 requirement
identifier were replaced with `[case id redacted]` and `[requirement id redacted]` before the file was
committed. Task, visibility, ordering, counts, timings, every validator line and the exit status are
preserved unchanged — the redaction removes which sealed case ran, never whether it passed.

Every one of the twelve files was passed through the same redaction, not just this one, so "needed no
redaction" is a checked result rather than an assumption; the other eleven needed none.

The file's own header records the substitution counts and the SHA-256 of the log exactly as produced,
before redaction. That digest identifies a payload a reviewer cannot read, so it is corroboration, not
evidence: what the claim rests on is the redacted log, which carries every fact except the sealed
identifiers. A reviewer with a checkout reproduces both by running the command.

## The scripts behind `08`, `09`, `10` and `11`

Throwaway verification, run from a scratch directory and deliberately not committed: a check this
packet ships would be payload in C, and these exist to inspect the payload rather than to be part of
it.

- `08` recomputes what `src/e0/identity.ts`, `src/e0/private-corpus.ts` and
  `src/identity/construction-policy.ts` already expose and `npm run validate:e0` already enforces.
- `10` re-derives, against an identity set no longer in the tree, the same failure that
  `tests/e0-freshness.test.ts` asserts against the current one. The committed control is the binding
  one; `10` only shows it has teeth.
- `11` calls `assessFreshness` with different corpora and reproduces round 3's enumeration, so the
  argument for the narrowing can be checked rather than taken on the report's word.

